    "lawCode": "Add §837-w, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7562\n\nSPONSOR: SALAZAR\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the executive law, in relation to the creation of a\n\"purple alert system\" for missing victims of domestic violence\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill creates a purple alert system to notify the public of missing\nvictims of domestic violence by unifying the resources of the Division\nof Criminal Services, Department of Transportation, appropriate state\nagencies to inform the public along with private or governmental enti-\nties who participate in the dissemination of urgent public information.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSECTION 1 amends the executive law by adding a new section 837-w with\nthe following provisions:\n\nSection 1 of the new section 837-w establishes a purple alert system to\nbe activated on behalf of a missing victim of domestic violence who has\nelected to participate in the alert system.\n\nSection 2 of the new section 837-w directs the commissioner of DOS to\ndesignate a statewide coordinator of the purple alert system and to\nadopt rules and directives to implement this system.\n\nSection 3 of the new section 837-w provides the framework as to how the\npurple alert system would function, including designating the state\nagencies and categories of private entities to be included.\n\nSection 4 of the new section 837-w provides that a law enforcement agen-\ncy shall notify the division if the agency receives a report regarding a\nmissing victim of domestic violence who is determined by the agency to\nbe a participant in the purple alert system.\n\nSection 5 of the new section 837-w establishes the process the division\nis to follow once notified by a law enforcement agency of a missing\nvictim of domestic violence who has elected to participate in the purple\nalert system.\n\nSection 6 of the new section 837-w provides specific guidance to the\ndepartment of transportation regarding the use of dynamic message signs\nin the statewide alert system.\n\nSECTION 2 establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn most missing persons cases, the first 48 hours are the most crucial.\nMissing victims of domestic violence are innately more at risk of not\nbeing successfully reunited with their loved ones.  According to New\nYork States' Amber Alert statistics, in nearly 7 in every 10 AMBER Alert\ncases, children are successfully reunited with their parents. And in\njust over 17 percent of cases, the recovery is a direct result of the\nAMBER Alert. A Purple Alert system is designed to provide much needed\nemergency awareness to missing victims of domestic violence once\nconfirmed by appropriate parties that such individuals may be in danger.\nThis Alert system would also take into account any identity protection\nmeasures on behalf of the victim and work to make sure the public is\nalerted without placing protected identities at risk.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
